Node.js Event Loop: Deep Dive

1. First, the big picture
    When you run:
        console.log("A");

        setTimeout(() => {
            console.log("B");
        }, 0);

        console.log("C");
    Output:
        A
        C
        B
    Why?
        Because JavaScript runs synchronous code first. The timer callback doesn't execute immediately; it gets handled asynchronously and runs later when the event loop gets to it.
    Think of Node like this:
            JavaScript Code
                ↓
            Call Stack
                ↓
        ┌──────────────┐
        │  Event Loop  │
        └──────────────┘
            ↓       ↓
        Timers    I/O
            ↓       ↓
        setTimeout  fs/network
                    ↓
                Callbacks


2. Call Stack
    The call stack executes JavaScript synchronously.
    Example:
        function one() {
            console.log("one");
        }

        function two() {
            console.log("two");
            one();
        }

        two();
    Execution:
        two()
        ↓
        console.log("two")
        ↓
        one()
        ↓
        console.log("one")
    Output:
        two
        one
    The stack follows LIFO — Last In, First Out.


3. Where does setTimeout go?
    Consider:
        console.log("Start");

        setTimeout(() => {
            console.log("Timer");
        }, 0);

        console.log("End");
    Execution:
        Step 1
        console.log("Start");
    Call stack:
        console.log("Start")
    Output:
        Start
        Step 2
        setTimeout(...)
    Node registers the timer.
    The callback does not immediately go onto the call stack.
        Step 3
        console.log("End");
    Output:
        End
        Step 4
    The event loop eventually allows the timer callback to execute.
    Output:
       Timer
    Final:
        Start
        End
        Timer


